{"severity":"CRITICAL","confidence":85,"path":"macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/AppStore+WorkroomStatus.swift","line":278,"category":"testing","summary":"No test proves endWrite(projectRoot:) ever fires after performCommit's async Task completes — not even in the ordinary case, let alone when AppStore is deallocated mid-write.","failure_scenario":"performCommit's Task closure does `guard let self else { return }` before reaching `self.endWrite(projectRoot: root)` (line 288). writingProjectRoots lives on the shared ProjectStore.shared singleton (used by every window's AppStore), not on this AppStore instance. If the window/AppStore is torn down (e.g. the user closes the window) while a commit is still in flight, the guard fires, endWrite never runs, and the project root's write-in-flight count is permanently incremented. Every future commit/fetch/push/pull for that project, in any window, is then refused forever with .failed(.locked(nil)) until the app restarts. AppStoreWritingProjectTests.swift only asserts the synchronous pre-Task marking (testPerformCommitProceedsWhenNoOtherWriteIsInFlight never awaits the Task or checks isWritingProject becomes false again), so this release path — and its dealloc failure mode — has zero coverage.","fix":"Add a test that awaits performCommit's completion and asserts isWritingProject(root) becomes false again afterward (both success and failure results), and a test that deallocates the AppStore (using an explicit, retained ProjectStore) mid-write and asserts the shared ProjectStore's writingProjectRoots count is still released — or restructure the cleanup so it does not depend on `self` surviving (e.g. capture `root`/the ProjectStore directly instead of gating the whole tail on `guard let self`).","fingerprint":"macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/AppStore+WorkroomStatus.swift:278:testing","specialist":"testing"}
{"severity":"CRITICAL","confidence":75,"path":"macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/RemoteStateModel.swift","line":360,"category":"testing","summary":"No test proves writeDidFinish still fires (and thus AppStore.endWrite still runs) when RemoteStateModel or its owning AppStore is torn down mid-action.","failure_scenario":"`actionTask`'s closure calls `self?.writeDidFinish?(projectRoot)` (line 360) using optional chaining on a weakly-captured RemoteStateModel. If RemoteStateModel is deallocated before the write completes (e.g. the window closes mid-push/pull), the call silently no-ops and AppStore.endWrite is never invoked; even if RemoteStateModel survives, the writeDidFinish closure itself closes over `[weak self]` AppStore (AppStore.swift ~line 414: `self.remoteState.writeDidFinish = { [weak self] root in self?.endWrite(projectRoot: root) }`), which no-ops the same way if AppStore already died. Either path permanently leaks the project's entry in the shared ProjectStore.writingProjectRoots, refusing every future write for that project across all windows. RemoteStateModelTests.swift's testWriteStartAndFinishBracketASuccessfulAction only proves the model's own closures fire while the model is alive — no test tears down the model or its AppStore mid-flight.","fix":"Add a test that starts a slow write, deallocates the RemoteStateModel (or its AppStore) before the write's Task resolves, and asserts the write-in-flight count on the retained, shared ProjectStore is still decremented afterward — or make the cleanup independent of the owning object's lifetime.","fingerprint":"macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/RemoteStateModel.swift:360:testing","specialist":"testing"}
{"severity":"INFORMATIONAL","confidence":55,"path":"internal/config/config.go","line":149,"category":"testing","summary":"withLock's 30s pathological-hang backstop and the general lock-acquisition-error branch have zero test coverage and no injection seam to test them quickly.","failure_scenario":"`context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 30*time.Second)` is hardcoded inside withLock; the only way to exercise the `err != nil || !locked` branch after a real timeout is to hold the lock for over 30 real seconds in a test, which no test does. lock_test.go's TestWithLockReleasedImmediatelyWhenHolderDies only proves the fast/no-error path; if a future refactor breaks the fallback-to-unlocked behavior on timeout/error, no test would catch it.","fix":"Make the timeout/retry-interval injectable (e.g. unexported fields on Config with test-only overrides) so a test can shrink it to milliseconds and assert withLock still runs fn() unlocked (and returns no error) when the context deadline is hit.","fingerprint":"internal/config/config.go:149:testing","specialist":"testing"}
{"severity":"INFORMATIONAL","confidence":45,"path":"macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/GhosttySurfaceView.swift","line":1099,"category":"testing","summary":"pollAccessibilityContent's actual notification-posting logic has no test coverage anywhere — only the unrelated password-leak safety property is tested.","failure_scenario":"`pollAccessibilityContent` (gated on NSWorkspace.shared.isVoiceOverEnabled, diffing against lastAccessibilityValue/lastAccessibilitySelectedText, and posting .valueChanged/.selectedTextChanged) is new logic with no unit or UI test proving: that a real content change triggers a notification, that an unchanged read does not re-post, or that accessibilitySelectedText() itself returns readSelectionText() correctly. TerminalAccessibilityUITests.swift's only test scopes itself explicitly to the echo-disabled safety precondition, not to whether the polling/notification mechanism functions at all.","fix":"Add a test (unit-level if GhosttySurfaceView's dependencies can be seamed, or an XCUITest otherwise) that changes terminal content and asserts a distinguishable accessibility change is observable, and one that repeats an unchanged read and asserts no duplicate notification fires.","fingerprint":"macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/GhosttySurfaceView.swift:1099:testing","specialist":"testing"}
{"severity":"INFORMATIONAL","confidence":40,"path":"macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/FileTreeModel.swift","line":104,"category":"testing","summary":"The new .interrupted branch's 'leave the existing tree/state untouched' contract is only tested at the static list() helper level, never through FileTreeModel itself.","failure_scenario":"No test in the suite instantiates FileTreeModel (`grep` for `FileTreeModel(` in WorkroomAppTests finds nothing) and drives reload()/activate() to prove that when the injected runner returns a signalled result, `roots` and `state` genuinely stay at their prior values rather than being cleared — the `case .interrupted: break` at line 104 is reachable only through the model's Task, which no test exercises.","fix":"Add a FileTreeModel-level test that seeds roots/state via a first successful load, then reloads with a runner stubbed to return a signalled CommandResult, and asserts roots/state are unchanged afterward.","fingerprint":"macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/FileTreeModel.swift:104:testing","specialist":"testing"}

